1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,817
An engineer cherry-picks the wrong
commit onto a release branch.

2
00:00:08,857 --> 00:00:13,243
A cherry-pick copies one commit's
change onto the current branch.

3
00:00:13,283 --> 00:00:18,417
He remembers a recipe: git reset --hard
ORIG_HEAD undoes the last operation.

4
00:00:18,457 --> 00:00:23,932
He runs it. Git answers with a commit
subject that is exactly the fix he meant to

5
00:00:23,972 --> 00:00:25,838
pick. It looks like success.

6
00:00:26,178 --> 00:00:30,587
It's not. The release branch
now shows the history of main.

7
00:00:30,627 --> 00:00:35,866
The commit that pins the dependencies
for the release is gone from it.

8
00:00:35,906 --> 00:00:42,200
The only visible sign is one missing file.
If nobody notices, the next release build

9
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:46,649
resolves unpinned dependencies.
Remember that missing file.

10
00:00:46,989 --> 00:00:52,029
The command did what it says. The engineer
believed that ORIG_HEAD means "before the

11
00:00:52,069 --> 00:00:56,927
last command". It means "before the last
reset, merge, rebase or am", on whatever

12
00:00:56,967 --> 00:00:57,895
branch that was.

13
00:00:59,735 --> 00:01:03,315
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:03,355 --> 00:01:07,826
In the previous video you learned the
eight-step method and used it on three

15
00:01:07,866 --> 00:01:11,980
accidents. Today, five more, all with
committed work, all recoverable.

16
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:15,304
Each is the same method with
a different ref to consult.

17
00:01:15,644 --> 00:01:20,040
A wrong rebase: the entry in the
branch reflog below "rebase (finish)".

18
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:25,226
A reflog is the local log of where a name
pointed. A bad merge: the entry below the

19
00:01:25,266 --> 00:01:29,725
merge line. Commits on the wrong
branch: the difference between main and

20
00:01:29,765 --> 00:01:34,848
origin/main. Detached HEAD, where you commit
on no branch: the HEAD reflog, or git

21
00:01:34,888 --> 00:01:38,035
fsck. A wrong cherry-pick:
the branch reflog again.

22
00:01:38,375 --> 00:01:43,198
Two of the five have a root-cause box, and
both boxes are about recipes that work in

23
00:01:43,238 --> 00:01:45,514
one situation and do damage in the next.

24
00:01:47,354 --> 00:01:52,026
return a branch to its state before
a rebase when ORIG_HEAD is stale;

25
00:01:52,366 --> 00:01:55,012
undo a merge that was not
pushed and one that was;

26
00:01:55,352 --> 00:01:57,966
move commits made on the wrong
branch to the right one;

27
00:01:58,306 --> 00:02:00,136
rescue commits left in detached HEAD;

28
00:02:00,476 --> 00:02:03,526
remove a wrong cherry-pick without
touching the commits around it.

29
00:02:05,366 --> 00:02:07,680
Three ideas decide every case today.

30
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:12,017
First: which log. The branch reflog has
one line per operation on that branch.

31
00:02:12,057 --> 00:02:16,157
A whole rebase is one entry, "rebase
(finish)", and the line below it is the tip

32
00:02:16,197 --> 00:02:20,297
from before the rebase. A merge is one
entry, and the line below it is where the

33
00:02:20,337 --> 00:02:24,489
branch was. That's the log you read when
the question is "where was this branch".

34
00:02:24,529 --> 00:02:27,388
The HEAD reflog is the only
log that sees detached HEAD.

35
00:02:27,728 --> 00:02:31,247
Second: published or not. Before a
push, you may move the branch back.

36
00:02:31,287 --> 00:02:34,654
After a push to a branch that others
have pulled, you don't rewind.

37
00:02:34,694 --> 00:02:38,009
You add commits, such as a revert,
which undoes an earlier commit.

38
00:02:38,049 --> 00:02:40,501
For a merge the textbook
gives a three-row table.

39
00:02:40,841 --> 00:02:45,446
The same split applies to a wrong cherry-pick
and to commits on the wrong branch:

40
00:02:45,486 --> 00:02:47,510
reset before the push, revert after.

41
00:02:47,850 --> 00:02:52,995
Third: the lowest-risk move. 🟡 CAUTION:
git reset --keep <id> moves the branch and

42
00:02:53,035 --> 00:02:57,737
the files in the same way as --hard,
and refuses if that would overwrite an

43
00:02:57,777 --> 00:03:01,784
uncommitted change. Use it wherever
you would have typed --hard.

44
00:03:01,824 --> 00:03:06,969
And prefer copying to moving: with commits
on the wrong branch, copy first, remove

45
00:03:07,009 --> 00:03:07,412
second.

46
00:03:07,752 --> 00:03:12,736
When is ORIG_HEAD acceptable? Only as the
very next command after the operation you

47
00:03:12,776 --> 00:03:15,823
want to undo, and only if
that operation writes it.

48
00:03:15,863 --> 00:03:20,848
git cherry-pick doesn't. A commit doesn't.
So a rebase followed by one commit, or a

49
00:03:20,888 --> 00:03:25,630
cherry-pick at any time, leaves ORIG_HEAD
pointing somewhere you didn't intend.

50
00:03:25,970 --> 00:03:30,782
Failure modes: recipes that count parents,
such as git reset --hard HEAD~1 to "undo

51
00:03:30,822 --> 00:03:35,341
the last merge". After a true merge,
HEAD~1 is the previous tip of the branch.

52
00:03:35,381 --> 00:03:40,250
After a fast-forward, there is no merge
commit, and HEAD~1 is the previous commit of

53
00:03:40,290 --> 00:03:41,829
the branch that was merged.

54
00:03:43,669 --> 00:03:48,105
Keep the picture from last time: the commits
are boxes that never left the vault,

55
00:03:48,145 --> 00:03:50,757
and each accident put a
card in the wrong place.

56
00:03:51,097 --> 00:03:55,955
Today add one refinement. Each branch has
its own ledger page, and HEAD has a diary.

57
00:03:55,995 --> 00:03:59,920
The ledger page of a branch answers
"where was this card yesterday".

58
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:04,818
The diary answers "where did I walk".
ORIG_HEAD is a sticky note with one box number

59
00:04:04,858 --> 00:04:08,899
and no heading: it doesn't say which
card it was copied from, or when.

60
00:04:09,239 --> 00:04:14,259
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: draw
a branch of three commits, then the same

61
00:04:14,299 --> 00:04:18,344
branch after a rebase. Do three of
those commits exist now, or six?

62
00:04:18,384 --> 00:04:19,320
Say it out loud.

63
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:29,850
Six. The model breaks in one place worth
saying: a rebase doesn't move boxes.

64
00:04:29,890 --> 00:04:34,911
It builds new boxes with new numbers
and leaves the old ones behind.

65
00:04:34,951 --> 00:04:40,568
Undoing a rebase means pointing the card
back at the old boxes, and anything

66
00:04:40,608 --> 00:04:44,140
committed after the rebase
sits on the new ones.

67
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:50,707
In four of the five, the repair line
contains commits that existed all along.

68
00:04:50,747 --> 00:04:52,008
Only the names moved.

69
00:04:53,848 --> 00:04:55,338
Six commits ahead of the release
branch where three belong.

70
00:04:55,678 --> 00:04:59,673
A commit was added after the rebase,
so ORIG_HEAD isn't the answer.

71
00:04:59,713 --> 00:05:04,372
Read the branch reflog. Predict which
entry is the tip from before the rebase.

72
00:05:04,412 --> 00:05:05,998
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

73
00:05:10,838 --> 00:05:19,856
The entry below "rebase (finish)":
hotfix/judge-timeout@{2}, which is eb1f140.

74
00:05:19,896 --> 00:05:24,966
Anchor it, and anchor
the present state too.

75
00:05:29,506 --> 00:05:34,150
Two refs now hold the two versions of
the branch. Nothing can be lost by what

76
00:05:34,190 --> 00:05:38,895
follows. Move only the commit that was
made after the rebase onto the old tip.

77
00:05:44,035 --> 00:05:50,112
Three commits on release/1.4 again: the
two old ones with their old IDs, and "Log

78
00:05:50,152 --> 00:05:55,851
judge latency" as 13305ef. A wrong rebase
is repaired with a correct rebase.

79
00:05:56,191 --> 00:05:57,012
Read the word "Fast-forward".

80
00:05:57,352 --> 00:06:00,965
Three work-in-progress commits are on
main, and there is no merge commit.

81
00:06:01,305 --> 00:06:09,857
ORIG_HEAD..HEAD lists what the merge brought
in. The merge was the last command and

82
00:06:09,897 --> 00:06:15,033
merge writes the slot, so
ORIG_HEAD is valid here.

83
00:06:20,573 --> 00:06:25,282
--keep and not --hard. The
experiment branch still exists.

84
00:06:25,322 --> 00:06:27,001
Only main moved back.

85
00:06:27,341 --> 00:06:32,527
Now the recipe from a tutorial,
in a second copy of the incident.

86
00:06:32,567 --> 00:06:39,279
Quick quiz, two options. Does main end up
at 0529d31, where it was before the merge,

87
00:06:39,319 --> 00:06:41,772
or at df44d03? Say it out loud.

88
00:06:54,612 --> 00:07:05,253
Option two, df44d03. After a fast-forward,
HEAD~1 is one commit back along the

89
00:07:05,293 --> 00:07:12,237
merged branch. Almost
everyone expects the old tip.

90
00:07:12,577 --> 00:07:17,288
The second recipe, git reset --hard
ORIG_HEAD, brings the third commit back: the

91
00:07:17,328 --> 00:07:19,248
first reset had written the slot.

92
00:07:27,788 --> 00:07:31,544
The line "merge exp/few-shot:
Fast-forward" is the accident.

93
00:07:31,884 --> 00:07:35,708
The line below it, main@{3},
is where main was before.

94
00:07:36,048 --> 00:07:48,345
origin/main..main is the precise name of
the misplaced work: the commits on main

95
00:07:48,385 --> 00:07:52,817
that the server doesn't have.

96
00:07:53,157 --> 00:07:57,492
Nothing is lost here. The commits
have the wrong name on them.

97
00:07:57,532 --> 00:07:59,327
Copy first, remove second.

98
00:08:10,967 --> 00:08:16,679
The order matters. If you reset main first,
the two commits are held by the reflog

99
00:08:16,719 --> 00:08:20,327
only, and you recover them
as origin/main..main@{1}.

100
00:08:20,367 --> 00:08:26,219
The lab does it in that order on purpose.
If the target branch doesn't exist yet, no

101
00:08:26,259 --> 00:08:31,621
copy is needed: create the branch at the
current commit, then move main back.

102
00:08:31,661 --> 00:08:36,742
And if the commits were already pushed
to a shared main, don't rewind it:

103
00:08:36,782 --> 00:08:41,161
cherry-pick them to the feature
branch and revert them on main.

104
00:08:41,501 --> 00:08:46,052
The marks of a detached session: a
"checkout: moving from main to" a tag or a

105
00:08:46,092 --> 00:08:51,060
commit, commits above it, and a "checkout:
moving from" forty hexadecimal digits "to

106
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:54,339
main" that ends it. There are
two sessions in this log.

107
00:08:54,379 --> 00:08:57,738
Reading reflogs of real length
for such patterns is slow.

108
00:08:57,778 --> 00:09:00,302
Predict what git fsck
--no-reflogs reports.

109
00:09:00,642 --> 00:09:05,174
Two dangling commits, tips that no ref
names, and the graph shows what hangs on

110
00:09:05,214 --> 00:09:05,463
each.

111
00:09:05,803 --> 00:09:08,977
A line of two hotfix commits,
and a single experiment.

112
00:09:15,517 --> 00:09:20,613
Each line has a name now. What happens to
the hotfixes next is an ordinary decision,

113
00:09:20,653 --> 00:09:22,264
made without time pressure.

114
00:09:22,604 --> 00:09:26,455
-x recorded the source, which makes
the mistake visible in review.

115
00:09:26,495 --> 00:09:30,937
The pick itself was clean, and that is
the dangerous case: a wrong pick that

116
00:09:30,977 --> 00:09:33,767
conflicts gets attention,
and a clean one ships.

117
00:09:34,107 --> 00:09:38,640
After it has finished and before a push,
step the branch back by one reflog entry

118
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:40,108
and pick the right commit.

119
00:09:45,448 --> 00:09:49,685
One step back, then the right pick,
5564cff. After a push to a shared release

120
00:09:49,725 --> 00:09:54,239
branch, revert the wrong commit instead.
And now the reflex from the opening, in a

121
00:09:54,279 --> 00:09:55,794
second copy of the incident.

122
00:10:03,334 --> 00:10:07,770
There's the opening again. The release
branch lands on the history of main, the

123
00:10:07,810 --> 00:10:11,453
pinning commit turns dashed, and
the reset writes the slot again.

124
00:10:19,293 --> 00:10:24,805
In the branch reflog, release/2.1@{2} is
the state before the wrong pick: e30eec3,

125
00:10:24,845 --> 00:10:30,358
"Pin dependencies for 2.1". The result of
the bad reset looked plausible, which is

126
00:10:30,398 --> 00:10:35,504
what makes this failure expensive. That's
the missing file from the opening.

127
00:10:37,344 --> 00:10:38,992
Five mistakes to watch for.

128
00:10:39,332 --> 00:10:45,387
git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD after a
cherry-pick, or a few commands after a rebase.

129
00:10:45,427 --> 00:10:51,709
Root cause: ORIG_HEAD is one slot written
by reset, merge, rebase and am; it holds a

130
00:10:51,749 --> 00:10:56,224
value from an earlier operation,
possibly on another branch.

131
00:10:56,564 --> 00:11:02,157
"Undo the last merge" with HEAD~1. Root
cause: a fast-forward creates no merge

132
00:11:02,197 --> 00:11:07,428
commit, so HEAD~1 is a commit of the
merged branch, not the previous tip.

133
00:11:07,768 --> 00:11:12,381
Resetting a rebased branch to its old
tip when work was added afterwards.

134
00:11:12,421 --> 00:11:17,226
Root cause: the new commit sits on the
rebased commits; anchor both tips and

135
00:11:17,266 --> 00:11:19,648
transplant the new commit with --onto.

136
00:11:19,988 --> 00:11:23,301
Moving main back before
copying the misplaced commits.

137
00:11:23,341 --> 00:11:28,394
Root cause: after the reset only the reflog
names them; copy first, remove second.

138
00:11:28,734 --> 00:11:32,240
Rewinding a shared branch to
remove a pushed merge or pick.

139
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:36,927
Root cause: others have the commits and
will bring them back or diverge; add a

140
00:11:36,967 --> 00:11:37,829
revert instead.

141
00:11:39,669 --> 00:11:44,322
Now, out of the lab. A platform team maintains
a release branch for an API gateway.

142
00:11:44,362 --> 00:11:49,015
On a Friday an engineer backports "the rate
limit fix" by cherry-picking the tip of

143
00:11:49,055 --> 00:11:53,313
main. The tip is a feature, per-tenant
quotas, and the pick applies cleanly.

144
00:11:53,353 --> 00:11:54,443
It's not pushed yet.

145
00:11:54,783 --> 00:11:59,575
A reviewer notices the -x line in the
message, which names a source commit that

146
00:11:59,615 --> 00:12:04,469
isn't the fix. The engineer opens the
reflog of the release branch, not of HEAD,

147
00:12:04,509 --> 00:12:09,545
takes the entry below the cherry-pick line,
and moves the branch there with --keep.

148
00:12:09,885 --> 00:12:13,687
Then he picks the right commit, again
with -x. Before pushing he compares the

149
00:12:13,727 --> 00:12:17,679
release branch with its state on the server
and sees exactly one new commit that

150
00:12:17,719 --> 00:12:21,771
changes one line. The whole incident is
four commands, and none of them could have

151
00:12:21,811 --> 00:12:23,218
overwritten uncommitted work.

152
00:12:23,558 --> 00:12:28,428
Had the pick already been pushed, the same
team's rule says: revert on the release

153
00:12:28,468 --> 00:12:32,321
branch, and explain in the revert
message which commit was meant.

154
00:12:34,161 --> 00:12:40,832
Your turn. Do Lab 12.4, "A wrong
rebase", in lab-manual/m12-recovery.md.

155
00:12:41,172 --> 00:12:45,286
Start from the symptom. Before you read
the reflog, predict how many entries the

156
00:12:45,326 --> 00:12:48,972
branch reflog has for the rebase, and
which selector names the old tip.

157
00:12:49,012 --> 00:12:53,281
Before the repair, draw the graph you expect
afterwards, with the number of commits

158
00:12:53,321 --> 00:12:54,734
ahead of the release branch.

159
00:12:55,074 --> 00:12:59,271
The challenge is Lab 12.6, "Commits on
the wrong branch", in the same file.

160
00:12:59,311 --> 00:13:03,790
It resets main first on purpose. Predict
the range expression that names the two

161
00:13:03,830 --> 00:13:04,863
commits afterwards.

162
00:13:06,703 --> 00:13:07,970
Pause and answer out loud.

163
00:13:12,810 --> 00:13:17,707
A strong answer starts with what ORIG_HEAD
is on disk and which commands write it,

164
00:13:17,747 --> 00:13:22,765
and names at least two common commands that
don't. From that it derives how the slot

165
00:13:22,805 --> 00:13:26,257
can hold a value from another
operation or another branch.

166
00:13:26,297 --> 00:13:31,315
It then gives the procedure, not a replacement
recipe: which log to read, which line

167
00:13:31,355 --> 00:13:36,252
to take, how to anchor before moving, and
which form of reset refuses to overwrite

168
00:13:36,292 --> 00:13:40,768
uncommitted changes. It closes with the
check that would have prevented the

169
00:13:40,808 --> 00:13:43,237
incident, which is one read-only command.

170
00:13:45,077 --> 00:13:48,004
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

171
00:13:48,344 --> 00:13:53,426
After a rebase, the old tip is the entry
below "rebase (finish)" in the branch

172
00:13:53,466 --> 00:13:56,905
reflog; work added later is
transplanted with --onto.

173
00:13:57,245 --> 00:14:01,594
An unpushed merge is undone by reflog entry
or by ORIG_HEAD immediately; a pushed

174
00:14:01,634 --> 00:14:02,569
merge is reverted.

175
00:14:02,909 --> 00:14:06,194
Commits on the wrong branch are
copied first and removed second.

176
00:14:06,534 --> 00:14:12,829
Detached work is in the HEAD reflog, and
git fsck --no-reflogs lists its tips.

177
00:14:13,169 --> 00:14:19,645
I check git log -1 ORIG_HEAD before every
use of it, and I prefer git reset --keep.

178
00:14:21,485 --> 00:14:28,593
Read the rest of section 13.8 in Chapter
13. Do Lab 12.5, "A bad merge", Lab 12.7,

179
00:14:28,633 --> 00:14:34,608
"Lost work in detached HEAD", and
Lab 12.9, "A wrong cherry-pick", in

180
00:14:34,648 --> 00:14:36,962
lab-manual/m12-recovery.md.

181
00:14:37,302 --> 00:14:41,264
Today you undid five accidents
by reading the right log.

182
00:14:41,304 --> 00:14:47,194
Do the wrong rebase lab before the next
video. Next time: uncommitted work, and the

183
00:14:47,234 --> 00:14:51,839
remote side. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

184
00:14:51,879 --> 00:14:53,554
See you in the next one.
